"Polynomial removal fails for ordered binary matrices (https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/Polynomial-removal-fails-for-ordered-binary-matrices-September-25-2026)\nciteturn3academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Date: Fri Sep 25 00:00:00 2026 ... This disproves the polynomial ordered binary matrix-removal conjecture.\nTitle: Polynomial removal fails for ordered binary matrices\nAuthors: OpenAI\nDate: Fri Sep 25 00:00:00 2026\n\nWe construct a fixed $66\\times66$ binary matrix for which ordered matrix removal has no polynomial bound. This disproves the polynomial ordered binary matrix-removal conjecture. Ordered copies preserve the separate row and column orders and match both zeros and ones; removal permits changing entries in either direction.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOrdered Matrix Removal Conjecture -- from Wolfram MathWorld (https://mathworld.wolfram.com/OrderedMatrixRemovalConjecture.html)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; The ordered matrix removal conjecture asks for polynomial quantitative bounds when eliminating a fixed binary matrix pattern from a larger binary matrix. ... Order 37, 83-101, 2020. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11083-019-09494-3.\n\n# Ordered Matrix Removal Conjecture\n\nThe ordered matrix removal conjecture asks for polynomial quantitative bounds when eliminating a fixed binary matrix pattern from a larger binary matrix. For a fixed Image: k×k pattern Image: H, a copy in an Image: n×n matrix Image: A uses increasing row indices and increasing column indices, independently, and matches every zero and one of Image: H. Write Image: N_H(A) for the number of these copies. The matrix is Image: epsilon-far from Image: H-free if at least Image: epsilonn^2 entries must be changed to eliminate every copy. The conjectural estimate has the form\n\nImage: N_H(A)>=c_Hepsilon^(C_H)n^(2k), \n\nwith positive constants depending only on Image: H.\n\n## References\n\nAlon, N. and Ben-Eliezer, O. \"Efficient Removal Lemmas for Matrices.\" Order 37, 83-101, 2020. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11083-019-09494-3.\n\nWeisstein, Eric W. \"Ordered Matrix Removal Conjecture.\" From MathWorld--A Wolfram Resource. https://mathworld.wolfram.com/OrderedMatrixRemovalConjecture.html--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPolynomial removal fails for ordered binary matrices (https://closedform.nishachay.tech/e/openai-math-2026-190/)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 3 days ago; > The polynomial ordered binary matrix-removal conjecture asserts that for each fixed $H$H there are constants $c_H,C_H>0$c H​,C H​>0 such that, for every integer $n\\ge1$n≥1, every $0<\\epsilon<1$0<ϵ<1, and every binary $n\\times n$n×n matrix $A$A, $$N_H(A)\\ge c_H\\epsilon^{C_H}n^{2k} \\quad\\text{whenever}\\quad \\operatorname{dist}_H(A)\\ge\\epsilon. ...   1. openai/math, Lean scope note for family 190 (2026) \"The formalized result disproves a polynomial removal bound for one explicit $66\\times66$66×66 binary pattern.\"\n\n# Polynomial removal fails for ordered binary matrices\n\nOne fixed 66×66 pattern of zeros and ones has no polynomial removal bound in ordered binary matrices.\n\n1 paper September 25, 2026 Explained by AI\n\nRemoval lemmas say a grid that is far from avoiding a pattern must contain many copies of it. For ordered grids of zeros and ones, people conjectured the number of copies falls only like a power of the distance. This paper builds one 66 by 66 pattern where that fails. The copies can be far rarer than any such power predicts.\n\nFirst step\n\n—\n\nSignificance\n\nSolid\n\nIt refutes a conjecture about property testing with an explicit pattern and construction.\n\n## The explanation\n\nTake a small grid of zeros and ones, two rows by two columns. Its top row reads 1, 0 and its bottom row reads 1, 1. Call it the pattern. Now take a 3 by 3 grid of ones, except a 0 in the top right corner. Where does the pattern appear inside it?\n\nA copy means choosing two rows, top to bottom, and two columns, left to right. The four cells where they cross must match the pattern exactly, zeros includedSource 1. The 0 must be the top right corner. Then either of the other two columns works, and either of the lower two rows. That gives 4 copies. Change that single 0 to a 1, and every copy disappears.\n\nGrids like this are called binary matrices. The row and column order matters, which is why these copies are called ordered. A matrix is ε-far from pattern-free if you must change at least a fraction ε of its cells to remove every copy. Changes may go either way, 0 to 1 or 1 to 0Source 2. A removal lemma says that a matrix far from pattern-free must contain many copies.\n\nThat qualitative statement was already known for ordered matrices, from Alon, Ben-Eliezer and FischerSource 3. The open question was how many copies it guarantees. Alon, Fischer and Newman raised the ordered questionSource 4. Alon and Ben-Eliezer later posed the polynomial versionSource 5. It predicts at least c·ε^C·n^(2k) copies in an n by n matrix, for a k by k patternSource 6. Here c and C are constants that depend only on the pattern. In words, the guarantee should shrink only like a power of ε.\n\nThis paper shows that this failsSource 7. It gives one fixed 66 by 66 pattern of zeros and ones. For each whole number h, it builds a square matrix n = (386h + 2)·2^h cells wide. For h = 1 that is 776. The matrix is far from pattern-free: at least one cell in (386h + 2)² must change. Yet its number of copies, divided by n^132, is at most that same fraction times 2^(−h). The fraction shrinks slowly as h grows, while 2^(−h) halves at every step. No fixed power of the fraction can keep up.\n\nThe difficulty is that removing copies can create new ones. A small set of cells can hit every existing copy. Here, all original copies pass through only 2^h cellsSource 8. But any repaired matrix must differ from the original in at least 4^h cellsSource 9. So changing those 2^h cells is never enough. The paper makes this happen by indexing rows and columns with a branching treeSource 10. Avoiding the pattern pushes conflicting values down the tree until they collide at its leaves.\n\nEarlier examples of this kind of failure needed a third symbol in the big matrix. This one uses only 0 and 1Source 11.\n\nHow sure is this? A computer proof checker called Lean has checked a formal version of the main result. The Lean scope note says it covers the explicit 66 by 66 pattern and the sequence of matrices. It requires every zero and one to match, and it allows edits in both directions. No specialist review of the paper has been published.\n\nWhat it doesn't show. This does not say how bad removal can get in general; it gives one pattern and one family of examples.\n\n## Where it came from\n\nThis result comes from an unreleased OpenAI model, published in the openai/math release on 6 October 2026.\n\n## Hype check\n\nSolid if it holds. Lean has checked the main statement for the explicit pattern; no specialist review is published.\n\n## Words used\n\nBinary matrix\n    A grid of zeros and ones.\n\nOrdered copy\n    A choice of rows and columns, in their original order, whose crossing cells match the pattern exactly.\n\nPattern-free\n    Containing no ordered copy of the pattern.\n\nε-far\n    Needing changes to at least a fraction ε of the cells before it becomes pattern-free.\n\nRemoval lemma\n    A rule that a grid far from pattern-free must contain many copies of the pattern.\n\nPolynomial bound\n    A guarantee that shrinks only like a fixed power of ε.\n\n## The original paper\n\n  * Polynomial removal fails for ordered binary matrices ↗ (PDF, opens in a new tab)\n\nSeptember 25, 2026 Formal proof listed\n\nLean notes ↗\n\n  1. 1\n\n> Thus a copy matches the zeros as well as the ones, and the row and column choices are independent.\n\nPolynomial removal fails