d columns indexed by a binary tree, with a plus block and a minus block at each internal node on each axis.\n\nPolynomial removal fails for ordered binary matrices Back to text\n  11. 11\n\n> These examples use a third symbol in the host matrix; our construction uses only $0$0 and $1$1.\n\n## Other sources\n\n  1. openai/math, Lean scope note for family 190 (2026) \"The formalized result disproves a polynomial removal bound for one explicit $66\\times66$66×66 binary pattern.\"\n  2. openai/math release notes (2026): model provenance and review status \"manuscripts and supporting proof artifacts produced by an internal OpenAI model\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe coarse connectivity witness conjecture — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/369886/the-coarse-connectivity-witness-conjecture)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; The coarse connectivity witness conjecture ... This proposes a coarse analogue of the connectivity-witness step in proofs of the classical grid theorem. ... coarse-graph-theory menger-theorem tree-width\n\n# \n\nThe coarse connectivity witness conjecture\n\nConjecture openOpen about 1 year · documented by 2025\n\nLet $K,n,m,r\\in\\mathbb{N}$K,n,m,r∈N. A graph is $(M,A)$(M,A)-quasi-isometric to a graph of tree-width at most $g$g in the usual coarse sense, and connected sets are at least $K$K apart when every pair of vertices from distinct sets has distance at least $K$K. Then there exist some $M,A,g\\in\\mathbb{N}$M,A,g∈N such that every graph with no $(M,A)$(M,A)-quasi-isometry to a graph of tree-width at most $g$g contains connected sets $U_1,\\ldots,U_n$U 1​,…,U n​ that are pairwise at least $K$K apart and such that for every pair $1\\leqslant i<j\\leqslant n$1⩽i<j⩽n, there exist no $m$m balls of radius at most $r$r hitting all paths between $U_i$U i​ and $U_j$U j​. Coarse connectivity witness conjecture.\n\nThis proposes a coarse analogue of the connectivity-witness step in 