AI’s original 66×66. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEﬃcient Removal Lemmas for Matrices (https://web.math.princeton.edu/~nalon/PDFS/testmatrixf4.pdf)\nciteturn3search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.4 years ago; property of two-dimensional matrices (where the row and column order is not ignored) over ... ordered matrix removal lemma: For any ﬁnite alphabet Γ, any hereditary property P of ... establish much more eﬃcient removal lemmas for several special cases of the above problem. ... δ > 0 polynomial in ϵ, such that for any binary matrix M in which less than a δ-fraction ... G-1347-304.6/2016 and the Simons Foundation.\nEﬃcient Removal Lemmas for Matrices\nNoga Alon∗\nOmri Ben-Eliezer†\nJune 1, 2019\nAbstract\nIt was recently proved in [Alon, Ben-Eliezer, Fischer; FOCS’17] that any hereditary\nproperty of two-dimensional matrices (where the row and column order is not ignored) over\na ﬁnite alphabet is testable with a constant number of queries, by establishing the following\nordered matrix removal lemma: For any ﬁnite alphabet Γ, any hereditary property P of\nmatrices over Γ, and any ϵ > 0, there exists fP(ϵ) such that for any matrix M over Γ that is\nϵ-far from satisfying P, most of the fP(ϵ) × fP(ϵ) submatrices of M do not satisfy P. Here\nbeing ϵ-far from P means that one needs to modify at least an ϵ-fraction of the entries of\nM to make it satisfy P.\nHowever, in the above general removal lemma, fP(ϵ) grows very quickly as a function\nof ϵ−1, even when P is characterized by a single forbidden submatrix.\nIn this work we\nestablish much more eﬃcient removal lemmas for several special cases of the above problem.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternational Congress of Mathematicians 2022 July 6–14 (https://www.mathunion.org/fileadmin/ICM/Proceedings/ICM2022/978-3-98547-564-3-volume-6.pdf)\nciteturn3search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.9 years ago; At a high level, the proof of Theorem 4.10 in [46] follows the framework of [9], but the fine details differ substantially. ... **Removal for ordered graphs and matrices. ... We should point out that Alon, Fischer, and Newman [5] obtained a polynomial bound for the *unlabeled* variant of the matrix removal lemma, that is, one where the order of rows/columns of \\(H\\) does not matter.\nUsing the methods discussed in Section 3 this can probably be reduced to a tower-type bound. But the following problem is still open.\n\n**Problem 4.11.** Obtain poly\\((1/\\varepsilon)\\) bounds for the matrix removal lemma.\n\nWe should point out that Alon, Fischer, and Newman [5] obtained a polynomial bound for the *unlabeled* variant of the matrix removal lemma, that is, one where the order of rows/columns of \\(H\\) does not matter. Equivalently, the result of [5] gives a polynomial bound for the induced removal lemma (see Section 3.2) in bipartite graphs. In this case the input \\(G\\) is an \\(n\\times n\\) bipartite graph, and \\(G\\) has an induced copy of a bipartite \\(H\\) on vertex sets \\(U_1,U_2\\) if it has an induced copy in which \\(U_1\\subseteq V_1\\) and \\(U_2\\subseteq V_2\\), or vice versa. This efficient removal lemma was instrumental in the results described in Section 4.2.\n\n**4.5. Arithmetic removal lemmas for linear equations and functions**\n\nConsidering the importance of the removal lemma, it is natural to ask if analogous results can be obtained in other settings. A notable example is the removal lemma for linear equations over groups obtained by Green [56]. A significantly simpler proof of Green’s result was obtained by Král’, Serra, and Vena [65] who derived it from the graph removal lemma. To state Green’s result, we need a few natural generalizations of the notions we used in the setting of graphs. Let \\(S\\subseteq [n]\\) be a set of integers, let \\(M\\) be an \\(\\ell\\times t\\) integer matrix, and \\(b\\in \\mathbb{N}^{\\ell}\\) an integer vector. We say that \\(S\\) is \\((M,b)\\)-free if there is no \\(x\\in S^t\\) satisfying \\(Mx=b\\), and say that \\(S\\) is \\(\\varepsilon\\)-far from being \\((M,b)\\)-free if we need to remove at least \\(\\varepsilon n\\) of its elements to\n\n4701    LOCAL-VS-GLOBAL COMBINATORICS--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEfficient Removal Lemmas for Matrices - Tel Aviv University (https://cris.tau.ac.il/en/publications/efficient-removal-lemmas-for-matrices-2/)\nciteturn3search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; It was recently proved in Alon et al. (2017) that any hereditary property of two-dimensional matrices (where the row and column order is not ignored) over a finite alphabet is testable with a constant number of queries, by establishing the following ordered matrix removal lemma: For any finite alphabet Γ, any hereditary property P of matrices over Γ, and any ϵ > 0, there exists f_{P} such that for any matrix M over Γ that is ϵ-far from satisfying P, most of the f_{P} × f_{P} submatrices of M do not satisfy P. ... In particular, we show the following, which can be seen as an efficient binary matrix analogue of the triangle removal lemma: For any fixed s × t binary matrix A and any ϵ > 0 there exists δ > 0 polynomial in ϵ, such that for any binary matrix M in which less than a δ-fraction of the s × t submatrices are equal to A, there exists a set of less than an ϵ-fraction of the entries of M that intersects every copy of A in M. ... German-Israeli Foundation for Scientific Research and Development  | G-1347-304.6/2016\n\n# Efficient Removal Lemmas for Matrices\n\n  * Noga Alon\n  * , Omri Ben-Eliezer^{*}\n\n^{*}Corresponding author for this work\n\n  * School of Computer Science and AI\n  * COMPUTER SCIENCE\n\n  * Princeton University\n\nResearch output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\n\n6 Scopus citations\n\n## Abstract\n\nIt was recently proved in Alon et al. (2017) that any hereditary property of two-dimensional matrices (where the row and column order is not ignored) over a finite alphabet is testable with a constant number of queries, by establishing the following ordered matrix removal lemma: For any finite alphabet Γ, any hereditary property P of matrices over Γ, and any ϵ > 0, there exists f_{P} such that for any matrix M over Γ that is ϵ-far from satisfying P, most of the f_{P} × f_{P} submatrices of M do not satisfy P. Here being ϵ-far from P means that one needs to modify at least an ϵ-fraction of the entries of M to make it satisfy P. However, in the above general removal lemma, f_{P} grows very quickly as a function of ϵ^{− 1}, even when P is characterized by a single forbidden submatrix. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA coarse block-cut tree theorem (https://www.newx.sg/paper/detail/d89769d4-966f-11f1-b84c-00163e10baa7)\nciteturn3search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: last month; This work investigates the large-scale connectivity structure of graphs within the framework of coarse geometry and proposes a coarse-grained decomposition analogous to the classical block–cutpoint tree. ... Leveraging tools from coarse geometry, tree decomposition theory, and the coarse Menger theorem, the authors prove that for any graph \\(G\\) and positive integer \\(d\\), there exists such a tree decomposition whose adhesion sets have weak diameter at most \\(3d + 2\\).\n\n📝 Abstract\n\nWe prove a coarse analogue of the classic fact that every graph can be decomposed along its cut-vertices into $2$-connected components. Precisely, we prove that for every graph $G$ and a positive integer $d$, $G$ admits a tree decomposition whose adhesion sets have weak diameter at most $3d+2$ so that no two vertices $u,v$ lying in the same bag can be separated by a set of weak diameter at most $d$ whose distance from $u$ and $v$ is more than $d$. By the Coarse Menger's Theorem for two paths, this condition admits also a dual formulation, phrased in terms of the existence of two paths that are far from each other and connect the vicinity of $u$ with the vicinity of $v$.\n\nProblem\n\nResearch questions and friction points this paper is trying to address.\n\ncoarse geometry\n\nblock-cut tree\n\ngraph decomposition\n\nweak diameter\n\ncoarse connectivity\n\nInnovation\n\nMethods, ideas, or system contributions that make the work stand out.\n\ncoarse block-cut tree\n\ntree decomposition\n\nweak diameter\n\nCoarse Menger's Theorem\n\nadhesion sets\n\n🔎 Similar Papers\n\nThe Expansion Problem for Infinite Trees\n\n2023-08-02 arXiv.org Citations: 0\n\n\nAffiliations 2\n\nU\n\nUniversity of Oxford\n\nU\n\nUniversity of Warsaw\n\nAuthors to Follow\n\nJ\n\nJúlia Baligács\n\nUniversity of Oxford\n\n[Button: Follow]\n\nVáclav Blažej University of Warsaw, Poland graph theoryparametrized complexity Follow\n\nJ\n\nJadwiga Czyżewska\n\nUniversity of Warsaw\n\n[Button: Follow]\n\nMichał Pilipczuk University of Warsaw Parameterized complexitygraph theory Follow\n\nE\n\nEvangelos Protopapas\n\nUniversity of Warsaw\n\n[Button: Follow]--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUpper bounds for ordered Ramsey numbers of forests and bounded-degree graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38828)\nciteturn3academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Date: Wed Sep 30 02:52:16 2026\n\nWe prove the following two upper bounds for ordered Ramsey numbers: (1) Every ordered forest $F$ on $n$ vertices satisfies $R_{<}(F,F)=O(n^{1+\\lceil\\logχ_{<}(F)\\rceil})$.\nTitle: Upper bounds for ordered Ramsey numbers of forests and bounded-degree graphs\nAuthors: Lior Gishboliner, Xiangyu Li\nDate: Wed Sep 30 02:52:16 2026\n\nWe prove the following two upper bounds for ordered Ramsey numbers: (1) Every ordered forest $F$ on $n$ vertices satisfies $R_{<}(F,F)=O(n^{1+\\lceil\\logχ_{<}(F)\\rceil})$. This in particular answers a question of Geneson, Holme